    "notes": "From a Strat-O-Matic product photo in its WordPress media library (media item 7386, 'cart_sets', 1000x675; sourceUrl is the item's public REST record, sourceImage the file): four 2014 cards on a grey table. Randy Choate's advanced card lies on top, completely uncovered, flat and in focus, about 500 px wide; at 3.6-5.5x every cell, glyph, underline and d20 range is readable. (J.P. Arencibia's advanced card in the same photo was rejected: the Choate card covers the fielder of two of its cells. Alberto Callaspo's and Buddy Carlyle's basic cards are mostly covered.) Read twice: (1) from 3.6x crops of each platoon grid, (2) from fresh 4.5-5.5x crops at different bounds; no differences. YEAR AND TEAM are not printed on the advanced side: the photo is of the 2014 set (Carlyle's visible stat box, 31 IP / 23 H / 5 BB / 28 SO / 2 HR, is his 2014 Mets line; Callaspo's, 40 BB / 50 SO / .290 SLG / .290 OBP, his 2014 Oakland line), and Choate pitched only for St. Louis in 2014. Markers: diamond ◆, downTriangle ▼, upTriangle △ (RHB col4 roll5 '△5-gb (ss) A' with an italic 'SI* 1 / lo (ss) 2-20' split on the two lines directly beneath, before roll 6; binding unambiguous), bullet ● after two strikeouts, a groundball and two flyballs, underlined class letters as 'underline:A'. Home runs print as N-HR. Validator CHECK (kept; transcription verified, a property of the card): combined-advanced errors H/PA .0392 and SO .0167 (side-by-side, as tests/test_platoon scores it: H/PA .0365, SO .0245; HR and BB fit within .0025). Choate was a 36-IP lefty specialist in 2014 (148 batters faced), and the card prints an extreme split: vs LHB 48 strikeout chances and 0.1 singles; vs RHB no strikeouts and 42.85 single chances. Against each matchup's average batter that implies H/PA .133 / SO .345 vs LHB and .352 / .101 vs RHB, more strikeouts and hits than his season totals, and no single LHB share fits both (H/PA wants 76% LHB, SO 49%), so it is not a misprinted percentage. Like Bruney 2008 (34 IP, tolerated for H/PA), tiny platoon samples make the combined rates noisy."
